  "checkpoint_step": 1750,
  "controls": "cycle_attach 2.50/2.51/2.48/2.57, ring_system_restate 0.65/0.80/0.65, bond_reorder 1.33/1.49/1.44/1.59 -- flat across all four categories in both lanes.",
  "distinct_transitions_consumed": 51615,
  "headline": "Three phenomena were superimposed and are now separated. (1) On the REAL lane seen_transition ~ unseen_sibling ~ unseen_source for every atom family, so there is no memorization of training molecules and no deficit on unobserved alternative successors -- the source-only cross-entropy objective learns a transferable edit law within its training population. (2) The synthetic atom strata show a 2.4-5.0 nat cliff from seen to unseen WITHIN the same population on 165-312 consumed rows: ordinary thin-stratum underexposure, which full epochs will fix. (3) The residual real-lane gap appears ONLY between unseen_source and held_out, both of which are unseen, so it has no exposure component and is a population effect at the validation-role boundary.",
  "minimum_cell": 40,
  "per_cell_cap": 600,
  "prediction": "Resuming to one or two epochs will substantially shrink the synthetic seen/unseen cliff and leave the real-lane held_out gap essentially unchanged, because unseen_source already scores like seen and more exposure cannot improve on that.",
  "reading": "Within a family the branching factor cancels, so categories are directly comparable. seen good / unseen_sibling bad means incomplete multi-successor exposure and motivates multi-positive supervision. seen good with unseen_source and held_out both bad means transferable features are not yet learned and more exposure may help. unseen_source good with held_out bad means a residual population or split mismatch.",
  "refuted_hypotheses": [
    "memorization of training molecules (seen ~ unseen_source on the real lane)",
    "suppression of unobserved alternative successors, i.e. Arm B's premise (unseen_sibling ~ seen_transition on the real lane)",
    "one-hot cross-entropy being incapable of learning a transferable law"
  ],
